Architecture plan for the suanpan app

Product scope
- Build a static Vite + React + TypeScript app for an interactive suanpan abacus.
- Model each rod as 2 heaven beads and 5 earth beads with a configurable rod count.
- Keep core state transitions and value computation pure so UI, unit tests, and E2E tests share the same behavior.

Module structure
- `src/main.tsx`: React entry point.
- `src/App.tsx`: top-level app composition, environment-derived default rod count, reset/readout wiring.
- `src/config/env.ts`: parse and validate Vite env values such as `VITE_DEFAULT_ROD_COUNT`.
- `src/model/suanpan.ts`: shared types, constants, state factories, rod validation, reset helpers, and bead interaction helpers.
- `src/model/value.ts`: pure per-rod digit and full-board base-10 value computation.
- `src/components/Abacus.tsx`: board shell composed from rods and model state.
- `src/components/Rod.tsx`: per-rod frame, rod line, heaven/earth bead groups.
- `src/components/Bead.tsx`: touch-friendly bead button with active/neutral positioning.
- `src/components/ValueReadout.tsx`: computed value display and reset control.
- `src/styles/index.css`: Tailwind base plus app-level layout styles.
- `src/test/`: unit test setup and model/value tests.
- `e2e/`: browser tests for bead interaction, readout, and reset.

Shared types and state decisions
- Represent board state as ordered rods from most significant to least significant digit for display and value computation.
- A rod stores counts of active heaven beads and active earth beads, constrained to `0..2` and `0..5`.
- Heaven beads contribute 5 each and earth beads contribute 1 each; a rod digit can therefore represent `0..15`, matching suanpan capability even if decimal readout may normally avoid values above 9.
- Bead clicks set a contiguous active count for that bead group: clicking a neutral bead moves it and nearer neighbors toward the reckoning bar; clicking an active bead clears it and farther active neighbors away from the bar.
- Validation should reject non-integer or out-of-range rod counts and normalize/guard inconsistent bead state at public helper boundaries.

Issue dependency map
- #1 establishes the Vite React TypeScript project structure.
- #2 and #3 depend on #1 for styling/tooling and environment configuration.
- #4 depends on #1 and creates the shared suanpan model contract.
- #5 depends on #4 for value computation and state helpers.
- #6 depends on #5 for model/value unit coverage.
- #7 depends on #4 for static board rendering from model data.
- #8 depends on #7 and #4 for bead rendering and active/neutral positioning.
- #9 depends on #8 and #5 for interaction-driven state updates.
- #10 depends on #5 and #9 for live computed readout and reset behavior.
- #11 depends on #7-#10 for responsive board and touch target polish.
- #12 depends on #3-#5 for env/model edge-case handling.
- #13 depends on #9-#10 for browser interaction tests.
- #14 depends on the complete app and test/build scripts for static hosting documentation.

Key design decisions
- Use Vite env variables only with the `VITE_` prefix and expose documented defaults in `.env.example`.
- Keep all numeric logic outside React components; components should receive state, derived positions, and event callbacks.
- Prefer CSS transforms for bead movement so the DOM structure remains stable for tests and accessibility.
- Render beads as buttons with accessible labels and pointer-friendly minimum sizes.
- Use Vitest for unit tests and Playwright for E2E once the app shell exists.
- Production output should remain a static `dist/` build suitable for any self-hosted static server.
